Fix malformed oversize put hanging in bitbucket (issue #48; upstream bug,
unfixed in v1.13 and on master).

In prot.c, dispatch_cmd()'s OP_PUT case evaluated
`if (body_size > job_data_size_limit)` before the trailing-garbage guard
`if (end_buf[0] != '\0')`. A well-formed oversize put correctly calls
skip() and replies JOB_TOO_BIG after discarding the body. A malformed
command line (trailing whitespace or extra arguments) has no body, so
the same skip() entered STATE_BITBUCKET and hung waiting for
body_size+2 bytes that never arrive. Subsequent pipelined commands
were swallowed into the bit-bucket.

Per the protocol specification (doc/protocol.txt), a command line with
the wrong number of arguments or trailing garbage must yield
BAD_FORMAT\r\n immediately, without attempting to read a body.

Validate command-line syntax before considering JOB_TOO_BIG so
malformed oversize puts return BAD_FORMAT\r\n and well-formed oversize
puts still skip the body and reply JOB_TOO_BIG.

--- a/prot.c
+++ b/prot.c
@@ -1322,16 +1322,16 @@
             return;
         }
         op_ct[type]++;
-
-        if (body_size > job_data_size_limit) {
-            /* throw away the job body and respond with JOB_TOO_BIG */
-            skip(c, (int64)body_size + 2, MSG_JOB_TOO_BIG);
-            return;
-        }
 
         /* don't allow trailing garbage */
         if (end_buf[0] != '\0') {
             reply_msg(c, MSG_BAD_FORMAT);
+            return;
+        }
+
+        if (body_size > job_data_size_limit) {
+            /* throw away the job body and respond with JOB_TOO_BIG */
+            skip(c, (int64)body_size + 2, MSG_JOB_TOO_BIG);
             return;
         }
 
